## requests vs httpx ###
While both requests and httpx are excellent, widely used HTTP clients for Python, the core difference comes down to concurrency and modern web standards.
Think of httpx as the modern, next-generation evolution of requests. It intentionally copied the API of requests so it feels completely familiar, but it rebuilt the engine underneath to support modern Python features. 

Which one should you choose?
    Stick with requests if: You are maintaining a legacy synchronous application, you don't need concurrency, or you are writing simple scripts where performance isn't a bottleneck. It is battle-tested and virtually bug-free.

    Switch to httpx if: You are building modern applications using frameworks like FastAPI, you need to make concurrent/parallel requests, you want to utilize HTTP/2 for better performance, or you rely heavily on strict type-checking in your CI/CD pipeline.


### response.raise_for_status() ####
Checks the HTTP status code of an API response and automatically raises an HTTP exception if the request failed.
    2xx Status Codes (Success): Does nothing. Your code proceeds to the next line smoothly.
    4xx Status Codes (Client Errors): Raises requests.exceptions.HTTPError (e.g., 404 Not Found, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden).
    5xx Status Codes (Server Errors): Raises requests.exceptions.HTTPError (e.g., 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway).

Why it's useful:
It saves you from writing manual status checks (like if response.status_code != 200:) after every HTTP request. 
If your request fails, it halts execution immediately rather than letting your code fail silently down the 
line with cryptic errors.

### request.Session() ######
Persists parameters, cookies, and TCP connections across multiple HTTP requests, whereas direct functions like requests.get() treat every call as an isolated, standalone event.

Key Capabilities of a Session:
    -> Connection Pooling: Reusing TCP connections prevents the overhead of creating a new handshake for every single API request. This makes batch testing or multi-step requests significantly faster.

    -> Automatic Cookie Handling: When an API response sets a cookie (like a session ID after login), requests.Session() automatically attaches that cookie to all subsequent requests without manual extraction.

    -> Default Configuration: You can configure headers, authentication, or query parameters once on the session instance rather than duplicating parameters in every request call.

    -> How Requests Merges Headers in Session:
    Non-conflicting keys are merged: If self._session.headers has Authorization and your endpoint passes headers={'accept': '*/*'}, the outgoing request sends both headers.
    Conflicting keys are overridden temporarily: If both define the same header (e.g., Content-Type), the per-request header wins for that single request only.
    Session state remains untouched: After the request completes, self._session.headers retains its original state.

Example: 
# Standard requests.get() — Manual header passing every time
requests.get("https://api.example.com/user", headers={"Authorization": "Bearer token123"})
requests.get("https://api.example.com/posts", headers={"Authorization": "Bearer token123"})

# Session approach — Headers & connections are reused automatically
session = requests.Session()
session.headers.update({"Authorization": "Bearer token123"})

session.get("https://api.example.com/user")   # Automatically uses the bearer token
session.get("https://api.example.com/posts")  # Reuses connection and token




